    "body": "Police arrested a 78-year-old Philadelphia woman last month after she blocked a delivery robot on a sidewalk, an act she described as a protest against the machines’ use of public space.\nWhat’s Related\nCarolyn Marvin , a retired University of Pennsylvania communications professor, received a disorderly conduct citation. Police said she refused repeated orders to move out of the robot’s path, according to reports by CNN and Philadelphia television station WPVI.\nMarvin told CNN she had stopped in front of delivery robots before, sometimes delaying them for 10 or 15 minutes. This was the first time she had been arrested for doing so.\n“I was under the impression that I had more rights than the robot did,” Marvin explained. She said her objections include potential job losses for delivery workers, sidewalk safety , and privacy concerns involving the robots’ cameras.\nA peaceful protest\nPolice told WPVI that witnesses called to report a disturbance involving Marvin and an Uber delivery robot. Officers said she ignored their commands to let it pass.\nMarvin described her actions as peaceful protest and said she believes they are protected free speech. She told WPVI: “I have been protesting them whenever I saw them by standing in front of them and preventing them from going forward.”\nUber Eats introduced robot deliveries in Philadelphia in March. CNN reported that the company did not respond to its request for comment.\nPennsylvania’s rules for delivery robots\nPennsylvania passed a law in 2020 that added personal delivery devices to its definition of pedestrians. The law covers ground-based machines that transport goods using remote control, autonomous driving, or both.\nThe law also places requirements on the machines. In pedestrian areas, they must yield to people walking and cyclists or safely navigate around them. Their speed is limited to 12 mph in those areas.\nMarvin plans to contest the disorderly conduct charge on First Amendment grounds, The Daily Pennsylvanian reported. At Penn, she taught a course called “History and Theory of Freedom of Expression.”",
